-### Parameters
-* **LPIPS Network:** AlexNet [S2, S7][S8, unofficial] or VGG variant [S5][S10, unofficial].
-* **LPIPS Weights:** Learned weights from the original LPIPS paper [S5].
-* **SSIM Window Size:** $11 \times 11$ Gaussian kernel with $\sigma=1.5$ [S3].
-* **Depth Scale ($s$):** Computed via alignment to match ground-truth depth range [S7].
-* **Evaluation Interval:** Every 300 iterations [S3].
-
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-* **Information Leakage:** In appearance-aware NeRFs, optimizing appearance embeddings on held-out pixels causes metrics to fail by memorizing the image rather than generalizing [S12]. Use split-image evaluation to prevent this [S12].
-* **Zero/Mean Embeddings:** Setting appearance embeddings to zeros or mean values results in structurally accurate but appearance-mismatched renderings, causing perceptual metrics to fail [S12].
-* **Depth Alignment:** Predicted depth must be scaled to match ground-truth range before computing depth errors [S7].
-* **Clamping:** Predicted RGB values must be clamped to $[0, 1]$ before LPIPS computation [S8, unofficial].
-* **FID Resampling:** FID is sensitive to resizing; use antialiased (area) interpolation at 128×128 resolution [S14].
+**Parameters:**
+*   **LPIPS Network:** AlexNet or VGG [S7][S10, unofficial].
+*   **LPIPS Normalize:** Boolean flag, typically `True` [S8, unofficial].
+*   **SSIM Window:** $11 \times 11$ Gaussian, $\sigma=1.5$ [S3].
+*   **Evaluation Interval:** Every 300 iterations for training progress [S3].
+*   **Frame Sampling:** Interval of 5 frames, skipping keyframes [S8, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Background Artifacts:** PSNR is sensitive to background pixels. Compute PSNR only on valid pixels (where $I_{gt} > 0$) to ignore background [S8, unofficial].
+*   **HDR Tonemapping:** Synthetic HDR data must be tonemapped ($I^{1/2.2}$) and clipped before metric calculation to match the dynamic range of ground truth [S1].
+*   **Depth Scale Ambiguity:** Optimized scenes are variable up to a 3D similarity; predicted depth must be scaled to match ground-truth range before computing depth error [S7].
+*   **Appearance Leakage:** In appearance-aware models, naive evaluation (e.g., zero embedding) may yield structurally accurate but appearance-mismatched renders, causing perceptual metrics to fail [S12]. Use split-image protocols to isolate geometry from appearance [S12].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Masked Metrics Evaluation](15886-masked-metrics-evaluation.md)
+Masked Metrics Evaluation restricts PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS, and FLIP computation to specific regions of interest defined by a ground-truth or loss mask. It differs from standard metric computation by decoupling object-level fidelity from scene-level completeness, allowing fair comparison between object-centric and scene-centric renders.
+
+### [FID Evaluation](18884-fid-evaluation.md)
+FID evaluates the quality of generated images by measuring the similarity between the distribution of real images and generated images in a high-dimensional feature space. It sets itself apart from pixel-wise metrics like PSNR or SSIM by assessing perceptual quality and diversity rather than exact pixel reconstruction, making it suitable for evaluating the visual fidelity of radiance field renderings. It builds on [Quantitative Evaluation Metrics](9928-quantitative-evaluation-metrics.md) by providing a distributional comparison rather than a per-pixel error metric.
+
 ### Split-image Evaluation Protocol
-An evaluation protocol for appearance-aware NeRF models that optimizes the appearance embedding on a subset of pixels to adapt scene appearance without directly optimizing held-out pixels, preventing information leakage while measuring perceptual similarity [S12]. It splits a ground-truth test image into left and right halves, optimizes the appearance embedding using only the left half, renders the right half, and calculates perceptual metrics on the rendered right half against the ground truth right half [S12]. Unlike the NRW baseline which encodes the entirety of a held-out image with an appearance encoder, this scheme isolates geometry from appearance and limits information leakage [S12]. Pitfall: Setting the appearance embedding to zeros or the mean of optimized embeddings results in plausible but structurally accurate renderings that fail to match ground truth appearance, causing perceptual metrics to fail [S12].
-
-### [Masked Metrics Evaluation](15886-masked-metrics-evaluation.md)
-A metric reporting protocol that computes PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS on both the full image and the valid-masked region to isolate visible reconstruction quality. It computes standard metrics over all pixels and then recomputes PSNR restricted to pixels inside the validity mask ($I_{gt} > 0$), reporting these values alongside global metrics.
-
-### FID Evaluation
-Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) evaluates how well the method captures the appearance distribution of images [S4]. It is computed on an empty background (value=0, gray) at 128×128 resolution, using antialiased resampling if resizing is needed, as FID is sensitive to this aspect [S14].
+An evaluation protocol for appearance-aware NeRF models that optimizes the appearance embedding on a subset of pixels to adapt scene appearance without directly optimizing held-out pixels, preventing information leakage while measuring perceptual similarity [S12]. Unlike the NRW baseline which encodes the entirety of a held-out image with an appearance encoder (capable of storing the image itself in high-dimensional spaces), this scheme isolates geometry from appearance and limits information leakage [S12]. Setting the embedding to zeros or mean results in plausible but structurally accurate renderings that fail to match ground truth appearance, causing perceptual metrics to fail [S12].
 
 ### Scaffold-GS Evaluation Metrics
-Reports SSIM, PSNR, LPIPS, and storage size for specific benchmark scenes. On Mip-NeRF360 scenes (bicycle, garden, stump, room, counter, kitchen, bonsai), SSIM scores are 0.705, 0.842, 0.784, 0.925, 0.914, 0.928, 0.946 respectively. PSNR scores are 24.50, 27.17, 26.27, 31.93, 29.34, 31.30, 32.70 respectively. LPIPS scores are 0.306, 0.146, 0.284, 0.202, 0.191, 0.126, 0.185 respectively. Storage sizes are 248, 271, 493, 133, 194, 173, 258 MB respectively [S13]. On Tanks&Temples/Deep Blending scenes (Truck, Train, Dr Johnson, Playroom), SSIM scores are 0.879, 0.857, 0.779, 0.774 respectively [S13].
+Specific performance benchmarks for Scaffold-GS on Mip-NeRF360 and Tanks&Temples/Deep Blending datasets. On Mip-NeRF360, SSIM scores range from 0.705 (bicycle) to 0.946 (bonsai), PSNR from 24.50 (zoom) to 32.70 (bonsai), and LPIPS from 0.126 (kitchen) to 0.306 (bicycle) [S13]. Storage sizes range from 133 MB (room) to 493 MB (stump) [S13]. On Tanks&Temples/Deep Blending, SSIM scores range from 0.774 (Playroom) to 0.907 (Dr Johnson) [S13].
+
+### Geometric and Masked Evaluation Metrics
+Extends image quality metrics (PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS) with geometric reconstruction metrics: Bi-directional Chamfer L2 distance (Ch), Normal Consistency (NC), and Volumetric Intersection over Union (IoU) [S14, unofficial]. Geometric evaluation involves generating a mesh via Marching Cubes on the SDF with a resolution of $256^3$ [S14, unofficial]. Rendering is restricted to the human bounding box for comparison against segmented ground truth [S14, unofficial].
+
+### 2D Render Evaluation Protocol
+A supplementary evaluation method conducting semantics on 2D renders to complement 3D metrics, following the protocol of LERF [S15]. It computes mean Intersection over Union (mIoU) and mean accuracy (mAcc) on all classes, including background and foreground classes, on subsets of benchmark datasets [S15].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-* **Time Complexity:** PSNR and SSIM are $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of pixels. LPIPS is dominated by the forward pass through the feature extractor [G].
-* **Memory Complexity:** LPIPS requires storing intermediate feature maps for all layers, which scales with image resolution [G].
-* **Performance:** Evaluated every 300 iterations during training [S3]. On KITTI reconstruction, NSG achieves PSNR 26.66, SSIM 0.806, LPIPS 0.186 [S6].
-* **Strengths:** PSNR provides pixel-level precision. SSIM captures structural information. LPIPS captures perceptual similarity [S1, S2, S4].
-* **Limitations:** PSNR is sensitive to pixel-level errors. SSIM can be sensitive to misalignment. LPIPS depends on a pre-trained network [S2, S5].
-* **When to Use:** Use PSNR and SSIM for quick checks and standard comparison. Use LPIPS for perceptual quality assessment, especially in appearance-aware NeRFs [S2, S5].
+*   **Time Complexity:** PSNR and SSIM are $O(H \cdot W)$ per image [S3]. LPIPS is $O(H \cdot W)$ but involves a forward pass through a deep network (VGG/AlexNet), making it significantly slower than PSNR/SSIM [S7][S10, unofficial]. FID requires feature extraction for all images and covariance computation, scaling with the number of images [S4].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal for PSNR/SSIM. LPIPS requires memory for the network weights and intermediate activations [S10, unofficial]. FID requires storing feature vectors for all real and generated images [S4].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On KITTI reconstruction, NSG achieves PSNR 26.66, SSIM 0.806, LPIPS 0.186 [S6]. On Mip-NeRF360, Scaffold-GS achieves PSNR up to 32.70 and SSIM up to 0.946 [S13].
+*   **Strengths:** PSNR/SSIM provide fast, interpretable pixel-level error. LPIPS correlates better with human perception. FID captures global distributional fidelity.
+*   **Limitations:** PSNR/SSIM are sensitive to background and misalignment. LPIPS is computationally expensive. FID does not provide per-image feedback.
+*   **When to Use:** Use PSNR/SSIM for rapid iteration and baseline comparison. Use LPIPS for perceptual quality assessment. Use FID for generative model evaluation. Use geometric metrics when 3D structure is critical [S14, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS are standard metrics in computer vision and image processing [S1, S2, S4]. They were adopted for NeRF evaluation to provide a multi-faceted view of rendering accuracy, balancing pixel-level precision with structural and perceptual similarity [S1, S3]. The use of LPIPS in NeRF evaluation was popularized by works like BARF and NeRF-W [S7, S12]. The split-image evaluation protocol was introduced to address the issue of information leakage in appearance-aware NeRFs [S12].
+Quantitative evaluation metrics for NeRF and radiance fields originated with the seminal NeRF paper [S5], which established PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS as the standard suite for novel view synthesis evaluation. Subsequent works introduced specialized metrics: FID for generative radiance fields [S4], temporal metrics (tOF, tLP) for dynamic scenes [S6], and geometric metrics (Ch, NC, IoU) for reconstruction accuracy [S14, unofficial]. The split-image protocol was introduced to address appearance leakage in unconstrained photo collections [S12].
 
